# The midnight pools (playtest 2, X13): what the six open places hold after curfew, and who is about. Most of the
# castle is sealed; what is open feels shady, and nights are riskier than days: a risky outcome queues
# `night_suspicion` at its own odds (M41). Illicit money (the ring, the high table, the stores, Grisk's parcels,
# stolen pages) is a third way to pay and pulls alignment toward the Hollow; hosting upstairs is legal, only risky. Harmless nights exist too.

# ---------------- The Gilded Newt, upstairs ----------------

@event newt_company
title: Company for the Evening
place: gilded_newt
slot: midnight
class: routine
tone: tense
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: cup
tags: money danger
scene: An upstairs room at a tavern after hours, card tables under low lamps, well-dressed guests laughing, a student in borrowed finery refilling a glass.
text: {The woman who runs the upstairs room looks you over. "The guests like good company. Laugh at the jokes, keep the glasses full, lose gracefully at cards. Nothing more. Guests who want more are shown the stair."|The upstairs room again: velvet, lamplight, guests from the capital who tip in silver.|You know the room now, and which guests to sit beside.} {Tonight it's a merchant's birthday.|Tonight, a card party for a visiting magistrate.|Tonight the guests are masked, for no reason anyone gives.}
> left: Work the evening
  challenge negotiate journeyman:
    win:
      gold +medium
      mood -tiny
      say: {You laugh in all the right places and lose at cards exactly as much as you're meant to. The tips are heavy, and so is your head on the way home.|A guest tells you the story of their life. You listen as if it mattered. Perhaps it did. The tip says it did.}
    lose:
      gold +tiny
      mood -small
      queue night_suspicion chance unlikely
      say: {A guest's hand wanders and doesn't take the hint. You leave by the back stair with a few coins and a cold feeling that lasts the week.|You say the wrong thing to the wrong magistrate, and are paid off early and shown the stair. Someone from the castle is drinking at the bar below.}
  if bonds(partner) > 0:
    queue night_partner_asks chance unlikely
> right: Not tonight
  mood -tiny
  say: {You turn the work down and walk home past the lit windows, a little poorer and quite sure of yourself.|"Another night, then," says the woman, writing something in a small book. You wonder what.}

@event newt_high_table
title: The High Table
place: gilded_newt
slot: midnight
type: menu
class: routine
tone: tense
repeat: 4
when: not flag(grisk_gone)
weight: 9
art: cards
tags: money danger
scene: A round table in a back room under a single green lamp, piles of coins, a goblin in a velvet waistcoat keeping the bank.
text: {Behind the upstairs room there's a back room, and in it the high table. Grisk keeps the bank. Nobody here plays for crowns alone.|The high table again. Grisk nods to you as if you were a regular. You are becoming one.|Grisk has kept your seat warm, he says. It is not a comforting thought.} {A first-year's familiar sits in a cage beside the pot.|Someone has staked a silver wand and is sweating about it.|Somebody's signed promise is in the pot, folded small.}
> purse: Stake your purse
  hint: Guile against the table.
  meet grisk
  alignment -tiny
  gold -medium
  challenge deceive adept unlikely wild:
    win:
      gold +large
      gold +medium
      mood +small
      say: {You read the table like a book you've already finished. The pot slides over, and Grisk applauds politely with two fingers.|Your nerve holds and theirs doesn't. You walk home heavy with silver and hollow with nerves.}
    lose:
      queue night_suspicion chance even
      say: {The cards turn on you, then the room does. You leave lighter, and a prefect's lamp is going round the courtyard.|You lose the pot and your temper with it. Grisk sympathises, with interest.}
> favour: Stake a favour to Grisk
  hint: Win big, or owe him a job.
  when: not flag(owes_grisk)
  meet grisk
  alignment -small
  challenge deceive adept- unlikely wild:
    win:
      gold +large
      say: You stake "one favour, to be named" and win. Grisk smiles, tears up the promise, and keeps the pieces. "Next time," he says.
    lose:
      flag owes_grisk
      mood -small
      queue grisk_names_favour in 1
      say: You lose, and Grisk folds your promise into his waistcoat pocket like a love letter. "I'll be in touch," he says. "Soon."
> health: Play the bone-dice
  hint: The old northern game. Win or bleed.
  alignment -tiny
  challenge combat adept- wild:
    win:
      gold +medium
      renown +tiny
      say: The bone-dice game is played with a knife between the fingers. You keep all ten and win the pot. Grisk calls you "dear heart" for the rest of the night.
    lose:
      health -medium
      gold +small
      say: You win the pot and lose a fair amount of skin from your left hand. Amaranth, in the morning, asks no questions, very loudly.
> leave: Watch one hand, then go
  say: {You watch a student lose a familiar on a bluff, and go home and hug yours, or your pillow.|You watch, learn and leave. Grisk raises his glass to your back.}

@event night_partner_asks
title: Where Were You?
trigger: queued
defer: off_campus
class: incident
tone: tense
art: heart
tags: romance
cast:
  partner: any where bond(partner) == "partner" and around(partner)
scene: Two students on a cold stone stair in the morning, one holding a tavern's silver token found in the other's coat.
text: {partner} is waiting on the stair with a silver token from the Gilded Newt in {their} palm. It fell out of your coat. "The upstairs room," {they} say{s}. "People talk. I'd rather hear it from you."
> left: Tell {them} everything
  spark partner -tiny
  rel partner +small
  mood -small
  say: You tell the truth: the glasses, the cards, the guests, the stair. {partner} is quiet a long time. "Thank you for telling me," {they} say{s} at last, and means it, and it still costs you both.
> right: "It's nothing. A card game."
  spark partner -small
  alignment -tiny
  say: {partner} looks at the token, then at you, and puts it in your hand. "All right," {they} say{s}. It isn't. For a week {they} sit{s} a little further away at supper.

@event grisk_names_favour
title: The Favour, Named
trigger: queued
defer: off_campus
when: not flag(grisk_gone)
class: incident
tone: tense
actor: grisk
art: chain
tags: money danger
scene: A goblin in a velvet waistcoat waiting in a library alcove, a folded promise held up between two fingers.
text: Grisk is waiting in the library alcove with your promise between two fingers. "The favour," he says. "A small one. A crate goes from the boathouse to the village. Tonight. Or, if you prefer, the price of the crate." He smiles. "I always offer a choice."
> left: Move the crate
  unflag owes_grisk
  flag grisk_contact
  alignment -small
  challenge deceive vs bram.guile +1:
    win:
      say: The crate clinks and, faintly, sings. You don't look inside. Grisk tears up your promise. "You're good at this," he says, which is the worst thing he could say.
    lose:
      gain watched
      mood -small
      say: Bram stops you on the jetty and lifts the tarp. Charm-wine. The crate is gone, the favour counts as paid, and Bram has your name.
> right: Pay him off
  unflag owes_grisk
  gold -large
  mood -small
  say: You count out the crowns and Grisk gives you back the promise with a small bow. It feels like buying your own name back. It is, more or less.

# ---------------- The boathouse ----------------

@event ring_on_yourself
title: Bet on Yourself
place: boathouse
slot: midnight
class: routine
tone: tense
repeat: 4
weight: 9
art: sword
tags: danger money
scene: A lantern-lit cellar under a boathouse, a chalk ring on wet flagstones, a bookmaker holding up a slate of odds, a crowd in scarves.
text: {The ring under the boathouse has a bookmaker now, with a slate. "Fancy yourself?" he says. "Put your money where your fists are."|The bookmaker knows your name and your odds, and they've shortened.|Your name is on the slate tonight before you've said you'll fight.} {The water slaps under the floor.|Somebody is collecting teeth in a jar, as a joke, probably.|The crowd is bigger than last time.}
> left: Bet your purse on yourself
  alignment -tiny
  gold -medium
  challenge combat adept-:
    win:
      gold +large
      gold +medium
      renown +tiny
      health -small
      say: {You win, bloodied and grinning, and the bookmaker pays out through his teeth.|The crowd roars. You collect your bet and your opponent's grudging handshake, and limp home rich.}
    lose:
      health -medium
      queue night_suspicion chance even
      say: {You go down in the third exchange, and your purse goes with you. On the way home a light is on in the prefects' corridor.|A bad loss: the bet, a tooth's worth of pride and most of your skin.}
  if met(lyra):
    queue lyra_ring_word chance unlikely
> right: Fight for the purse only
  challenge combat adept-:
    win:
      gold +small
      health -small
      say: {A clean win for a small purse. Nobody's rich, nobody's hurt much.|You win, bow to your opponent and leave before the bookmaker can talk you into more.}
    lose:
      health -medium
      say: {You lose, bow, and spend the walk home finding new bruises.|A loss, and the loser's purse, which is nothing at all.}

@event lyra_ring_word
title: Lyra Has Heard
trigger: queued
defer: off_campus or not around(lyra)
class: incident
tone: tense
actor: lyra
art: sword
tags: social
scene: A proud red-haired student blocking a corridor with folded arms, a duelling glove tucked in her belt.
text: Lyra blocks the corridor. "The ring under the boathouse." She says it as if it were a smell. "Brawling for coins in a cellar, in front of drunks. You can actually duel. I've seen you. This is beneath you." A pause. "I'd hate to lose to someone who does it."
> left: "Then spar with me instead."
  rel lyra +small
  health -tiny
  say: She stares, then almost smiles. "Tomorrow, the Duelling Hall, six sharp." She beats you, and then shows you why, which from Lyra is close to an apology for the corridor.
> right: "It pays. You wouldn't know."
  rel lyra -medium
  gold +tiny
  say: Her face shuts like a door. "No," she says. "I wouldn't." She walks off. Later the Newt's landlady hands back the deposit you left on your bar tab: Lyra has paid it off. That is somehow worse.

@event grisk_pickup
title: A Parcel at the Boathouse
place: boathouse
slot: midnight
class: routine
tone: tense
repeat: 5
weight: 12
actor: grisk
when: flag(grisk_contact) and not flag(grisk_parcel) and not flag(grisk_gone)
art: pouch
tags: money danger
scene: A dark boathouse jetty at midnight, a small goblin holding out a parcel wrapped in oilcloth, the lake black behind him.
text: {Grisk is waiting on the jetty, a parcel in oilcloth under his arm. "Into the village by tomorrow afternoon. A cobbler's back door. Don't open it, don't drop it, don't be seen."|The jetty, the oilcloth, Grisk's teeth in the dark. "Same arrangement," he says.|Grisk again, and a heavier parcel than last time.} {It ticks faintly.|It's cold enough to frost your gloves.|It smells of oranges and something under the oranges.}
> left: Take the parcel
  meet grisk
  alignment -tiny
  flag grisk_parcel
  queue grisk_delivery in 1
  say: {You take it. It is heavier than it looks, and the walk back to your room is very long.|You hide it under your bed. You don't sleep well with it there.}
> right: Not this month
  meet grisk
  mood -tiny
  say: {"Next month, then," says Grisk, as if it were already arranged. He tucks the parcel away. You feel watched all the way back up the lawn.|Grisk shrugs. "There's always someone." You don't like how true it sounds.}

@event grisk_delivery
title: The Cobbler's Back Door
trigger: queued
slot: afternoon
defer: off_campus
class: incident
tone: tense
art: pouch
tags: money danger
scene: A narrow village lane in daylight, a student with an oilcloth parcel under one arm walking past a prefect who is buying apples.
text: {~You carry Grisk's parcel down to the village in broad daylight.|Grisk's parcel, the village lane, and too many people about.|The parcel is in your satchel and the satchel is suddenly the heaviest thing you own.} Bram is at the apple stall. The cobbler's back door is past him.
> left: Walk past as if you belong
  unflag grisk_parcel
  challenge deceive vs bram.guile +1:
    win:
      gold +large
      alignment -tiny
      say: {~You nod to Bram, buy an apple, and knock twice at the cobbler's door. A hand takes the parcel; another gives you a purse. Your heart takes an hour to slow down.|The door opens before you knock. Coins, a nod, done. You eat the apple on the way home and taste none of it.}
    lose:
      gain watched
      flag grisk_angry
      mood -small
      queue grisk_cross in 1
      say: {~Bram calls your name. You drop the parcel in a horse trough and walk on, very calmly. Bram follows you halfway home.|You panic at the corner and leave the parcel behind a water butt. When you go back, it's gone, and Bram is writing in his notebook.}
> right: Leave it at the door and run
  unflag grisk_parcel
  gold +small
  say: {~You go the long way round, drop it on the cobbler's step and run. The cobbler pays you, short, for the lack of manners.|You don't knock, and you don't wait. Short pay, slipped under your door that night.}

@event grisk_cross
title: Grisk Is Not Pleased
trigger: queued
class: incident
tone: grim
actor: grisk
art: chain
tags: money danger
scene: A goblin in a velvet waistcoat sitting on a student's bed uninvited, a ledger open on his knee, the dormitory window open behind him.
text: Grisk is sitting on your bed. You didn't leave the window open. "A parcel was lost," he says pleasantly, turning a page in his ledger. "Lost things have a price. I've written it down. Would you like to see?"
> left: Put it on your account
  meet grisk
  unflag grisk_angry
  if has(in_debt):
    debt_paid = debt_paid - 2
    mood -small
    say: "Two more months," he says, and writes it neatly. "Such a good customer." He leaves by the window. You don't sleep with it open again.
  else:
    gain in_debt
    debt_paid = 0
    debt_missed = 0
    mood -small
    say: He writes your name in the ledger under a new line: one parcel, six payments. "Twelve a month," he says, "as usual." He leaves by the window. You don't sleep with it open again.
> right: Tell him you're done
  meet grisk
  unflag grisk_angry grisk_contact
  flag grisk_gone
  health -small
  renown -tiny
  say: "Everyone is, eventually," Grisk says. On your way to breakfast, the stair you always take collapses three steps under you. Nobody saw. Everybody heard. You never see him again.

# ---------------- The refectory kitchens ----------------

@event kitchen_stores
title: The Stores
place: refectory
slot: midnight
class: routine
tone: tense
repeat: 4
weight: 9
art: meal
tags: money danger
scene: A dark storeroom behind a castle kitchen, sacks of sugar and crates of oranges stacked high, a student with an empty satchel.
text: {Behind the kitchens is the storeroom, full of sugar, spices and oranges from the south. The village pays well for oranges in winter, and nobody counts the crates.|The storeroom again. You know which crate is at the back and never counted.|The stores have a new padlock, which is mostly decorative.} {A mouse watches you without judgement.|The kitchen cat does judge you.|Somewhere a pot is still dripping.}
> left: Fill a satchel to sell
  alignment -small
  gold +small
  queue night_suspicion chance even
  say: {Oranges, a cone of sugar, a jar of saffron. The village grocer asks no questions and pays in coppers. Somebody in the castle will be short of saffron.|A full satchel and an empty conscience, or nearly. On the way out, the scullery door bangs behind you.}
> right: Take one orange and go
  mood +tiny
  say: {You eat it in the dark by the banked fire. It's the best orange you have ever eaten, and you know exactly why.|One orange. You leave the peel in the fire so nobody finds it.}

@event kitchen_night_baker
title: The Night Baker
place: refectory
slot: midnight
class: routine
tone: warm
repeat: 4
weight: 9
art: meal
tags: nature
scene: A warm castle bakehouse at midnight, a flour-dusted old baker kneading dough by the light of an open oven, a student leaning in the doorway.
text: {The bakehouse behind the kitchens is lit. The night baker, flour to the elbows, looks up. "Hungry or helpful?" she says. "Both is allowed."|The night baker waves you in without looking up. "Dough's waiting."|The bakehouse smells of tomorrow's bread. The baker has saved you a heel of yesterday's.} {The oven sighs.|A tray of rolls is cooling by the door.|She is humming something old.}
> left: Knead until the oven's hot
  lose hungry
  health -tiny
  mood +small
  say: {You knead until your arms shake. She gives you the first roll out of the oven and no lecture at all.|An hour of flour and warmth, and a hot roll for your trouble. You go back to bed with your hands smelling of bread, and sleep like a stone.}
> right: Just sit by the oven
  lose hungry
  mood +tiny
  say: {You sit by the oven with a roll from the cooling tray while she works. She doesn't ask why you're up. You don't ask why she is.|You eat the heel of bread and warm your feet. Upstairs, the castle is cold; down here, it never is.}

# ---------------- The Restricted Section ----------------

@event restricted_page_buyer
title: A Buyer for Old Pages
place: restricted_section
slot: midnight
class: routine
tone: eerie
repeat: 3
weight: 8
when: met(grisk)
art: book
tags: money danger mystery
scene: A dim Restricted Section aisle at midnight, a student holding a razor over an illuminated page, a black book watching from the shelf above.
text: {Grisk mentioned, once, that a collector pays for illuminated pages "of a certain age". The Restricted Section is full of them.|{flag(sold_page): There's a gap on the shelf where the last page you sold came from.|The illuminated book you read last time is back on its shelf, untouched.}|The illuminated books lean away from you as you pass.} {A gold-leaf dragon glints on one page.|One margin is full of tiny stars, all black.|A page here is worth a month's board.}
> left: Cut a page
  flag sold_page
  alignment -small
  gold +medium
  queue night_suspicion chance even
  say: {One clean cut. The book doesn't cry out, which is somehow worse. The collector pays well, and never asks where.|You sell the page and keep the guilt, which no one will buy.}
> right: Read it instead
  lore +small
  mana -tiny
  say: {You read the illuminated page by moonlight. It's a recipe for honey cakes, in exquisite gold leaf, and the gilding draws at your mana the whole time you read. You leave it where it is.|The page is a list of stars, by name, and each name pulls a little magic out of you as you read it. You learn three, and put the book back.}

# ---------------- The observatory roof ----------------

@event midnight_roof_partner
title: The Roof, Together
place: observatory
slot: midnight
class: routine
tone: warm
repeat: 4
weight: 10
cast:
  partner: any where bond(partner) == "partner" and around(partner)
art: star
tags: romance nature
scene: Two students lying side by side on an observatory roof at midnight under a sky full of stars, one blanket between them.
text: {You've brought {partner} up to the observatory roof, with a stolen blanket and two cups of cocoa going cold.|{partner} knows the way up now, and goes first.|{partner} has brought the blanket this time.} {The stars are very close.|A meteor, then another.|The castle below is all asleep.}
> left: Stay until the sky turns
  spark partner +small
  health -small
  queue night_suspicion chance unlikely
  say: {You name the stars between you, wrong, and argue about it, and stay until the sky goes grey. The stair down is lit by a prefect's lamp you just avoid.|You fall asleep up there and wake to the first bell, frozen and happy.}
> right: An hour, then back
  rel partner +small
  mood +tiny
  say: {One hour, one blanket, back in bed before anyone stirs. You can still feel where {partner} was leaning on you.|You go down early. {partner} squeezes your hand on the stair, which is better than any star.}

@event kael_midnight_roof
title: Kael at Midnight
place: observatory
actor: kael
slot: midnight
class: routine
tone: warm
repeat: 4
weight: 12
art: snow
tags: social
scene: A northern student sitting on the observatory parapet at midnight with frost spreading from his hands, the lake far below, the sky thick with stars.
text: {Kael is on the observatory parapet, which he shouldn't be, with frost spreading from his fingers into the stone. He moves over to make room.|Kael again, on the parapet, the night's cold not touching him.|Kael has left room on the parapet before you arrive.} {"I can't sleep south of the mountains," he says. "Too warm."|He is watching the lake, not the stars.|"Northern stars," he says, pointing at nothing you can see.}
> left: Sit with him
  meet kael
  rel kael +small
  health -tiny
  say: {You sit until your fingers ache from the cold. He names northern constellations in a language that sounds like snow falling. It's the most he's ever said.|He doesn't talk. Neither do you. When you go, he says "Goodnight," as if it were difficult, and it was.}
> right: Ask what he's watching for
  meet kael
  lore +tiny
  rel kael -tiny
  say: {"For it to wake," he says, and then shuts up like a book. You've pressed too hard. But you know which way he faces now.|"Nothing," he says, too fast. He's watching the middle of the lake. You leave him to it, colder than when you came.}

# ---------------- The gargoyle passage ----------------

@event corvin_note_was_mine
title: Second Timetable
place: gargoyle_passage
actor: corvin
slot: midnight
class: milestone
tone: light
when: seen(night_lead_in) > 0 and rel(corvin) >= 6
art: map
tags: social mystery
scene: A secret passage behind a gargoyle at midnight, a grinning dark-haired student holding up a thumbnail-sized silver map, chalk marks on the wall behind him.
text: In the gargoyle passage, Corvin holds up a map no bigger than a thumbnail. "Recognise it? The castle has a second timetable. It starts at midnight." He grins. "Who did you think writes notes like that? Prefects?"
> left: "Why me?"
  meet corvin
  rel corvin +medium
  mood -tiny
  say: "Because you looked at that door at the back of the library the way I do." He shrugs, and doesn't joke. "And because it's lonely, knowing all the doors." You don't sleep much, after.
> right: "You could have signed it."
  meet corvin
  rel corvin +small
  guile +tiny
  say: "And spoil it?" He tucks the map into your pocket. "Keep it. The doors move. The map knows." You check it in the morning, and one of the six doors has moved.

@event corvin_midnight_watch
title: Corvin Keeps Watch
place: gargoyle_passage
actor: corvin
slot: midnight
class: routine
tone: light
repeat: 4
weight: 10
art: lantern
tags: social
scene: A dark corridor at midnight, a dark-haired student leaning out of a niche to wave someone past, a prefect's lantern turning a far corner.
text: {Corvin leans out of a niche as you pass. "Bram's on the east stair. Take the west. You owe me nothing, I'm a saint."|Corvin's in his niche again with an apple and a list of where every prefect is.|"West stair," says Corvin, before you ask.} {He looks tired.|He has a new scratch on his cheek.|He's whistling, which he shouldn't.}
> left: Keep him company
  meet corvin
  rel corvin +small
  health -tiny
  say: {You share his apple and his niche. He tells you which tapestry hides a cupboard full of confiscated wands. You're up till the bells.|You keep watch with him for an hour. He's funnier at midnight, and sadder, and pretends to be neither.}
> right: Take the west stair
  meet corvin
  guile +tiny
  say: {The west stair is dark and empty, as promised. Corvin's map is never wrong. You wonder who taught him to be so careful.|You slip past Bram's lamp with a corridor to spare.}

# ---------------- The lakeshore ----------------

@event midnight_swim
title: The Lake at Midnight
place: lakeshore
slot: midnight
class: routine
tone: warm
repeat: 5
weight: 10
art: wave
tags: nature
scene: A moonlit lakeshore at midnight, clothes folded on a rock, a swimmer's head and a ring of ripples in the silver water.
text: {season == "winter": The lake at midnight has a skin of ice at the edges and stars in the middle.|{~The lake at midnight is black and silver and perfectly still.|Nobody swims after curfew, which is why you do.|The water is warm from the day and the air is not.}} {clock(hollowstar) >= 5: Far out, a ring of frost forms and fades.|{~An owl crosses the moon.|Somewhere on the far shore, a lantern.|A fish jumps, very loudly.}}
> left: Swim out to the middle
  mood +medium
  health -small
  say: {~You swim until the castle is a toy and the stars are all around you. The cold gets into your bones and stays till breakfast.|You float on your back in the middle of the lake. Something far below hums, once. You swim back faster than you swam out.}
> right: Paddle, then get dry
  mood +small
  health -tiny
  say: {~Knee-deep, then waist-deep, then out, gasping and laughing at yourself. Your teeth chatter all the way up the lawn.|A quick, cold paddle, and back to bed glowing.}

@event grey_on_the_shore
title: Someone Else Who Can't Sleep
place: lakeshore
slot: midnight
class: story
tone: eerie
when: clock(hollowstar) >= 2 and seen(hs_grey_stranger) > 0 and not flag(grey_shore_seen)
weight: 14
art: mask
tags: mystery hollowstar danger
chain: hollowstar
scene: A moonlit lakeshore at midnight, a tall man in a grey coat sitting on an upturned boat, frost spreading from his boots across the pebbles.
text: The man in grey is sitting on an upturned boat at the water's edge, as if he'd been waiting for a ferry. "You can't sleep either," he says pleasantly. "Nobody sleeps well near this lake. Sit. I won't keep you long. I only want to tell you what the headmistress won't."
> left: Sit and listen
  flag grey_shore_seen
  alignment -tiny
  lore +small
  mood -small
  say: He tells you, kindly, that the founders were frightened people who buried what they couldn't understand, and that it has been lonely down there for three hundred years. It sounds true. You walk back with it, and can't put it down.
> right: Walk away
  flag grey_shore_seen
  alignment +tiny
  health -tiny
  say: You turn your back and walk, and the cold follows you all the way up the lawn like a dog. At the door you look back. The boat is empty, and frosted white.
